verdicts.reasoning:
I checked each claim against the repo and every one holds up. The issue is only in the wording of the document. Nothing is computed wrongly.

(1) The §0 table at benchmarks/PREREGISTRATION_v9.md:27 still files "GCN with no edges" under Controls. Yet lines 34 and 37-42 select gcn_nograph+stack as the "best data-only" method. Line 27 was the same in the draft (a794dab), so the amendment in ee28470 never updated the table.

(2) Line 96 (not 97) calls `stack_lowrank` "the best data-only stack". That phrase is carried over from PREREGISTRATION_v8.md:24. In v9 it is false, and it was already false in the draft. v9_selection.json shows stack_lowrank at 1.0773, against mice+stack at 1.0275 and gcn_nograph+stack at 0.9917.

(3) The timeline matches the reviewer's account:
- the draft was committed at 23:06:58;
- the control dev panel (dev_panel_devctl_fast.json) was written at 23:12:23;
- the amendment ee28470 was committed at 23:23:33;
- the final prereg 0062e02 was committed at 23:34:37;
- holdout_runs.log shows derks2022 starting at 23:34:53.
No holdout output exists before the amendment.

(4) Effect on the code: none. confirm_v9.py:118 and :121 name stack_lowrank explicitly, and they read best_panel and best_graph from the frozen selection file. The wrong labels therefore change no test.

(5) I recomputed the reviewer's counterfactual from results/confirm_v9/derks2022.json. Mean RMSE of revised divided by mice+stack is 0.9753 under MCAR and 0.9569 under MNAR. Against gcn_nograph+stack it is 0.9902 under MCAR and 0.9758 under MNAR. The single H18 failure on derks2022 (11/12) is "MCAR revised vs gcn_nograph+stack all", at -0.98%. That just misses the -1% SESOI. Against mice+stack the margin would be about 2.5%, so that test would very likely have passed. I did not recompute the bootstrap CI. The amendment therefore made H18 harder, and H16 fails on gcn+stack regardless. The reviewer's framing is accurate.

verdicts.corrected_impact:
The "bpca" comparator is mislabelled. On 20 of 24 dev splits (every dataset except petrosius2023) it returns per-protein mean imputation exactly. The cause is that _ppca's ARD (baselines.py:195) runs in the cells-as-observations orientation, with n=30 against p of about 2500, and shrinks every component to zero whenever the low-rank signal is only moderate. As a result:
- the frozen dev scores for bpca (1.1487) and bpca+stack (1.0726) are effectively mean and mean+stack wherever it collapsed;
- the preregistered panel (PREREGISTRATION_v9.md l.25) claims a BPCA comparator it does not actually contain;
- any per-holdout claim that the champion beats BPCA is really a claim that it beats mean imputation, on any holdout where the same collapse occurs.

I would call this high severity (an invalid named baseline) rather than critical. There is no evidence that it changes best_panel (gcn_nograph+stack, 0.9917) or the comparator frozen for H16/H18. On the reviewer's two splits, the correctly oriented BPCA averages about 2-3% worse than the champion, so it is unlikely to beat 0.9917, though this has not been tested. The fix is either to implement BPCA in Oba's orientation (proteins as observations) or to relabel or drop the entry and say so in the results.
